{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Discovered while fixing the ubuntu/py3.11 hang (89d5-61da-b621-47f8): _build-and-test.yml's\nshared suite carries `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread`, but every OTHER CI pytest\ninvocation has NO per-test hang guard. `timeout-minutes` only bounds a whole job, so one\ndeadlocked test hides behind the last `-q` dot with no traceback and no culprit named — and\nnone of the affected lanes declares `timeout-minutes` at all, so they run under GitHub's\n360-minute default.\n\nTriage named six unguarded lanes; a sweep of .github/workflows found two more\n(`_eval-discipline`, `_optionality`). All eight are fixed here so the class is closed.\n\n## Operator decision applied\n\nThe operator decision (2026-08-10): per-lane budgets calibrated from historical runtime data,\ngenerous allowance for live-service variance; do NOT copy one blanket timeout across lanes.\nApplied as an auditable rule:\n\n**Per-test budget = at least 10x the lane's observed mean per-test duration, floored at 300s.**\n\nDurations came from recent successful Actions runs, reading each pytest step's\n`started_at`/`completed_at` (runs 30986591446, 30964805133, 31003041092, 30983268038).\n`--timeout-method=thread` is used everywhere for the same load-bearing reason as the shared\nsuite: the default `signal` method arms SIGALRM in the main thread and cannot fire while a\nworker is blocked in a C-level flock/socket/subprocess call — exactly the hang shape these\nlanes exercise.\n\n| Lane | Observed evidence | Budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| ext-integ `external-llm` (live LLM matrix) | 16 tests, step 138-870s (bedrock 347->870s) | 900 |\n| ext-integ `external` (live Jira Cloud) | 19 tests, step 85-120s | 600 |\n| ext-integ `langfuse-trace` | 1 test, 24-29s | 300 |\n| ext-integ `jira-dc-harness` | 63 tests, 2384-2635s (~41s/test mean) | 900 |\n| structured-output-baseline | 1 test drives the whole sweep, 65-173s | 3600 |\n| test.yml `external` | no run history (see below) | 600 |\n| _eval-discipline | offline unit tests | 300 |\n| _optionality degradation-path | offline unit tests | 300 |\n\nThree budgets need a basis beyond the rule:\n\n- **test.yml `external`** has NEVER executed (gated on a `workflow_dispatch` input never fired;\n  its credential env keys are all commented out), so it has no history. It takes the same 600s\n  as external-integration.yml's `external` lane, which runs the same `-m external` selection.\n- **structured-output-baseline** is a SINGLE test driving the whole sweep, sized by the dispatch\n  input `max_calls` (default 300 live calls). A budget sized to the observed 173s would truncate\n  a deliberately larger human-dispatched sweep, so it gets 3600s — 20x the worst observation.\n- **jira-dc-harness**'s 900s also clears by 3x the in-test field-readiness wait\n  (`JIRA_DC_FIELD_READY_TIMEOUT`, default 120s, raisable to 300s per\n  tests/external/live_jira_dc/README.md), which runs INSIDE a test and is charged to the\n  per-test budget.\n\n## Dependency availability\n\nSeven of eight lanes need no dependency work: `pytest-timeout==2.4.0` is pinned in the `dev`\nextra (pyproject.toml) and each installs `--extra dev`. The exception is `_optionality.yml`'s\ndegradation-path step, which runs in a deliberately minimal `/tmp/clean` venv installing only\n`pytest jsonschema mcp` so `agents_extra_installed()` stays False. `pytest-timeout` is added to\nthat install line rather than carving a permanent exemption into the new parity assertion: it\nis a test-harness tool like the `pytest` already installed there, it is not part of the agents\nstack whose ABSENCE the lane proves, and it installs in a LATER step than the clean-core-wheel\n`find_spec` assertions — so what the lane proves is unchanged.\n\n## Anti-regression\n\n`tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py` asserts the guard only against `_build-and-test.yml`\nvia `_pytest_suite_run_blocks`, which filters on `\"pytest -m\" in run and \"--dist\" in run` —\nblind twice over: `--dist` excludes every non-xdist lane, and `pytest -m` misses both\n`python -m pytest` and a bare `pytest tests/...`. It is replaced by an exhaustive sweep over\nEVERY .github/workflows file, with the detection rule specified here rather than left to\nimplementation choice.\n\n**Detection rule.** Parse each workflow, walk `jobs.*.steps[]`, take each step's `run` body,\nsplit into lines and DISCARD any line whose first non-whitespace character is `#` (a shell\ncomment; YAML comments never survive parsing). On each surviving line, match a pytest command\ntoken at a command boundary:\n\n    (?:^|\\|\\s*|&&\\s*|;\\s*)\\s*(?:[\\w./-]*/)?(?:python[\\d.]*\\s+-m\\s+pytest|pytest)\\b\n\nStart-of-line or a `|`/`&&`/`;` separator, an optional path prefix, then `python -m pytest` or\n`pytest` — independent of `-m` and of `--dist`. A matching step is a pytest lane and MUST carry\nboth `--timeout=` and `--timeout-method=thread` somewhere in that body (flags may sit on a\ncontinuation line, so the assertion is against the whole body). Checked against the four forms\nthat occur and the near-misses it must NOT claim:\n\n| Text | Detected? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pytest -m \"external and llm_live\" ... -q` | yes | bare token at line start |\n| `pytest tests/external/test_structured_output_matrix.py -q` | yes | line start, no `-m` |\n| `python -m pytest -q \\` | yes | `python -m pytest` form |\n| `/tmp/clean/bin/python -m pytest \\` | yes | path-prefixed `python` |\n| `# --timeout (pytest-timeout) is the ... GUARD` | no | comment line, discarded |\n| `/tmp/clean/bin/pip install pytest pytest-timeout` | no | argument to pip, not at a command boundary |\n\nThe last row is load-bearing: `_optionality.yml` installs pytest by name on a `pip install`\nline, so a bare-substring matcher would demand a timeout flag on an install step.\n\nAlongside the universal assertion the sweep carries a liveness one: the discovered lane set\nmust COVER a known-expected set spanning all four invocation forms, so a refactor that breaks\nthe matcher fails loudly instead of passing vacuously over zero lanes. New lanes need no test\nedit — they simply must be guarded.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\nIn external-integration.yml, each step gains `--timeout-method=thread` plus:\n\n- [ ] AC1: `pytest -m \"external and llm_live\"` carries `--timeout=900`.\n- [ ] AC2: `pytest -m \"external and not llm_live\"` carries `--timeout=600`.\n- [ ] AC3: the Langfuse trace step carries `--timeout=300`.\n- [ ] AC4: the Jira DC harness step carries `--timeout=900`.\n- [ ] AC5: structured-output-baseline.yml's pytest step carries `--timeout=3600\n      --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC6: test.yml's `pytest -m external` step carries `--timeout=600\n      --timeout-method=thread`.\n- [ ] AC7: _eval-discipline.yml and _optionality.yml's pytest steps each carry\n      `--timeout=300 --timeout-method=thread`, and _optionality.yml's clean venv installs\n      `pytest-timeout` so the flag is honored instead of erroring as an unknown option.\n- [ ] AC8: tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py implements the detection rule above and fails\n      if ANY pytest-invoking step in ANY .github/workflows file lacks either flag. Proven by\n      removing a flag from a lane of EACH of the four forms in turn (`pytest -m` in\n      external-integration.yml, bare `pytest <path>` in structured-output-baseline.yml,\n      `python -m pytest` in _eval-discipline.yml, path-prefixed in _optionality.yml) and seeing\n      a failure each time; passes on the committed tree.\n- [ ] AC8b: the sweep does NOT flag non-invoking text — _optionality.yml's `pip install pytest\n      pytest-timeout` line and _build-and-test.yml's `# --timeout (pytest-timeout)` comment are\n      not lanes — and its discovered set covers all four forms, so a broken matcher cannot pass\n      vacuously.\n- [ ] AC9: each per-lane budget and its evidence is recorded as a YAML comment at the step it\n      governs, so the number is auditable at the point of use.\n- [ ] AC10: workflows stay valid — `make lint` (runs actionlint) and `make typecheck` pass, and\n      no lane's selection, markers, or behavior changes beyond the added flags."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786502567769620001,"uuid":"6fc09c30-79bc-451e-800c-5c824530a91b"}